/// Chapter 18 of the guide, written from md-docs/about/introduction.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page AboutWhy(path: "/about/why", title: "Why WebFluent", description: "Why WebFluent exists: one meaning per word, a compiler that refuses what would fail in the browser, and one source for sites, PDFs and slides.", layout: DocsShell("Why WebFluent", kind: .explanation, chapter: t("nav.group.about"), blurb: "Why the language exists, and the choices it makes: one meaning per word, a compiler that refuses what would break in the browser, and one source for the web and for paper.", source: "md-docs/about/introduction.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "what-breaks-a-website", label: "What breaks a website"), Anchor(id: "one-lexeme-one-meaning", label: "One lexeme, one meaning"), Anchor(id: "refuse-what-would-fail-in-the-browser", label: "Refuse what would fail in the browser"), Anchor(id: "secure-by-construction-not-by-advice", label: "Secure by construction, not by advice"), Anchor(id: "one-source-for-the-web-and-for-paper", label: "One source for the web and for paper"), Anchor(id: "fine-grained-with-no-virtual-dom", label: "Fine-grained, with no virtual DOM"), Anchor(id: "one-binary", label: "One binary"), Anchor(id: "what-it-gives-up", label: "What it gives up"), Anchor(id: "where-to-go-from-here", label: "Where to go from here")], nextTo: "/about/how-reactivity-works", nextLabel: t("ch.19"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This essay argues for WebFluent: why a language for websites, PDFs and slide decks should exist when JavaScript frameworks already do the job, and what it gives up to be what it is.\n\nThe argument in one line: a website is a program whose mistakes are found by its readers, and most of those mistakes can be found by a compiler first, if the language is designed so that the compiler can see them.")

        Heading("What breaks a website", id: "what-breaks-a-website").h2

        Markdown("Here is a page with a counter, the smallest program that has every part a site has: an address, text, state, and something the reader does.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "A first WebFluent page.") {
    state count = 0
    Container {
        Heading("Hello, world").h1
        Text("You have clicked {count} times.")
        Button("Click me").primary { on click { count = count + 1 } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Now count the ways the same page goes wrong in a typical stack. A class name misspelled in the markup does nothing. A prop a component does not take is ignored. A field read from a record that does not have it is `undefined`, and the page shows `undefined`. A value that may be `null` crashes the page only for the reader whose data has a gap. A link to a route that was renamed is a 404 that nobody sees until a reader follows it. None of these is a hard problem. Each one is a problem the toolchain had all the information to report, and did not.\n\nWebFluent starts from the other end. Every word in a program resolves to one declaration the compiler knows: a built-in in the registry, or a component, store, type or page the project declares. A word that resolves to nothing is an error, not a no-op.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "A first WebFluent page.") {
    Button("Save").primry
}"#, title: "wf · draws E103")

        Markdown("The build stops on that line, says that `Button` has no flag `primry`, and lists the ones it takes. The browser never sees the page.")

        Heading("One lexeme, one meaning", id: "one-lexeme-one-meaning").h2

        Markdown("The compiler can only check what it can read, so the grammar is built for reading. Each kind of word means one thing:\n\n- A lowercase keyword declares: `page`, `component`, `store`, `state`, `derived`, `action`, `type`, `enum`, `api`.\n- A capitalised name is an element or a type: `Button`, `Todo`.\n- `.primary` after a call sets a `Bool` prop or picks the case of an enum prop, and nothing else.\n- `name: value` passes a prop. A block is the element's children. `on click \{ \}` is a handler. `style \{ \}` is CSS.\n\nThere is no second spelling for any of these, no attribute that is sometimes a prop and sometimes markup, no string that is sometimes a template. That is a constraint on the author, and it is the reason the compiler, the linters, the formatter and the language server can all read one registry and agree. When the editor offers you the flags of a `Button` after a dot, it is reading the same table the build refuses `primry` from.\n\nThe cost is real: you cannot invent syntax, and a library cannot extend the grammar. A component you write is called exactly as a built-in is, and that is the extension point the language offers.")

        Heading("Refuse what would fail in the browser", id: "refuse-what-would-fail-in-the-browser").h2

        Markdown("A compiler that stopped at spelling would be a linter. WebFluent's goes further, because most of what fails in a browser is a type error or a reactivity error in disguise:\n\n- A value that may be `null`, read as if it were not, is `T04`. An `if let`, a `??` or a `!= null` check narrows it.\n- A `state` that reads `now` once and so never ticks is `X06`; the fix the compiler offers is `derived`.\n- An effect that writes what it reads, and so never settles, is `X02`.\n- A link to a path no page answers is `R01`.\n- A `Secret` shown, logged, spliced into a URL or kept with `persist` is `T12`.\n- A page the build's own Content-Security-Policy would block is `E902`; the build reads its output back and holds it to the policy it ships.\n\nEach finding has a code, a place, a hint and, where there is one right answer, a fix the editor applies. The bet is that a project with no findings is a project whose pages load, and `wf verify` exists to test the bet: it opens every route in a real browser and fails on an uncaught error, a failed request or a page that drew nothing.\n\nTypes make most of this possible, and they are optional. A value the checker cannot work out is `Any`, which agrees with everything, so a program that declares no types checks as it always would. Every annotation you add narrows what the checker can say.")

        Heading("Secure by construction, not by advice", id: "secure-by-construction-not-by-advice").h2

        Markdown("The same idea applies to security. Advice such as \"escape your output\" or \"never put a token in `localStorage`\" is agreed to in every review and hard to follow on every line. WebFluent turns the advice into properties of the output: every string an element shows is a text node; markup goes in only through `Unsafe.Html`, which is named so a review finds it; an `href:` whose scheme a browser would run is refused where it is written; an `env` name that is not public cannot be read from a page. The [security essay](/about/security) lists each guarantee and what is still yours.")

        Heading("One source for the web and for paper", id: "one-source-for-the-web-and-for-paper").h2

        Markdown("A team that publishes a site usually publishes documents too: an invoice, a report, the slides for the quarter. These tend to live in a second toolchain, with a second set of styles that drifts from the first.\n\nWebFluent lays a PDF out from the same HTML and CSS the page would get, by its own paged engine, with no browser and no external tool. A `Card` in a PDF looks like the `Card` on the site, a theme's tokens reach both, and a `Presentation` makes a deck from the same components. The same templates render with JSON data from Rust or Node on a server. One language, one look, one set of checks.")

        Heading("Fine-grained, with no virtual DOM", id: "fine-grained-with-no-virtual-dom").h2

        Markdown("A `state` is a signal. What reads it is what redraws: the text node that splices `\{count\}`, an attribute, a branch. Nothing re-renders a component to find out what changed, so there is no virtual DOM to diff and no memo to forget. [How reactivity works](/about/how-reactivity-works) follows a click from the handler to the text node it changes.")

        Heading("One binary", id: "one-binary").h2

        Markdown("`wf` is the compiler, the dev server, the formatter, the test runner and the template renderer; its sibling `wf-lsp` is the language server, built from the same compiler. There is no `node_modules`, no bundler, and no configuration to write before the first page. A build writes only the runtime modules the program reaches and only the component rules it uses, so a page of text ships a few kilobytes of script. The [essay on what a build ships](/about/what-a-build-ships) shows the numbers.")

        Heading("What it gives up", id: "what-it-gives-up").h2

        Markdown("A language with opinions is the wrong tool for some work:\n\n- **A framework's ecosystem.** There is no package of WebFluent components to install from npm. Any browser library can be used from a plain script under `src/` or a `meta.scripts` entry, but you write the binding yourself.\n- **Rendering per request.** A WebFluent site is static files and a browser runtime; your server stays your server. The template engine renders HTML and PDFs with data on a server, but it runs no handlers after the render.\n- **Freedom of syntax.** One spelling for each thing means none of your own.\n\nIf those costs are acceptable, what you get in return is a site whose mistakes the compiler finds before your readers do.")

        Heading("Where to go from here", id: "where-to-go-from-here").h2

        Markdown("- To learn the language, start with [Your first page](/learn/01-getting-started).\n- Coming from a framework, read [Coming from React](/coming-from/react) (or Vue, or Svelte) first.\n- To look a fact up, use the [reference](/reference).\n- This guide describes WebFluent 6. [Upgrading](/about/upgrading) lists what each release changed.")
    }
}
